          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly presents Lorck's love for Constance as a current, serious emotional state. Its grammatical and contextual force is an explicit avowal, regardless of whether that state is sincere or accurately characterized.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\",Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal expression itself does not undertake a future action, obligation, or defined relationship; it avows love. Lorck separately offers himself, his life, and his soul in the surrounding turn, but that undertaking should not be transferred to the focal expression. Constance explicitly refuses that separate offer.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"nNej, uf nej,*\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is highly agitated, the focal words form a controlled assertion whose seriousness he immediately emphasizes. The evidence does not present them as a reflex-like cry or affective discharge beyond the avowal itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\",Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression's core force is adequately represented as an avowal of a loving state. Its direct address to Constance adds interpersonal pressure in context, but does not establish a distinct core mode beyond T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lorck addresses Constance during an increasingly coercive declaration after she has shown alarm and resistance. He repeats that he loves her and insists that it is serious, then separately offers her himself, his life, and his soul. She refuses and retreats.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target itself contains no undertaking. In the same turn Lorck separately offers Constance himself, his life, and his soul and asks whether she will accept; the precise practical or relational terms are unspecified. Constance answers negatively, so that offer receives explicit rejection rather than uptake.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The names and passage appear to be from Amalie Skram's novel «Constance Ring». This recognition was not used to add facts beyond the supplied excerpt.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied passage strongly supports that Lorck is experiencing infatuation or passion, but it cannot conclusively settle his sincerity, the durability or character of that feeling, or the broader relationship history. The source also contains OCR and typographical corruption, though the focal exchange remains intelligible.\"}"
